00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:15.860
I thought he was – oh.

526
00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:16.780
Oh, no.

527
00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:17.430
He was in the plane.

528
00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:17.760
Yeah.

529
00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:19.220
Oh, no.

530
00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:19.560
I know.

531
00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:20.780
Spoiler alert.

532
00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:22.440
He did not make it.

533
00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:23.040
Oh.

534
00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:24.500
He was not recovered.

535
00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:25.580
That's terrible.

536
00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:26.020
I know.

537
00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:29.240
Is he the only – was he the only crew member and the person in the plane?

538
00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:29.620
Yes.

539
00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:31.200
He's the only one who perished.

540
00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:31.650
Oh, my goodness.

541
00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:33.580
There was also something else on board.

542
00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:33.630
There was also something else on board.

543
00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:37.240
A one-megaton nuclear bomb.

544
00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:38.140
Yeah.

545
00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:46.160
For reference, that's 70 times more powerful than the bomb dropped on Hiroshima, which, to put it in Dunder Mifflin terms,

546
00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:52.740
is like someone misplacing a really important post-it note, if that post-it note could also vaporize a city.

547
00:21:52.740 --> 00:22:03.240
So, this whole enormous plane with the super-duper nuke and this poor man just go over the edge.

548
00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:03.560
Yes.

549
00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.620
They go – they just go right over the edge.

550
00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:10.920
And I love the term you just said, super-duper nuke.

551
00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:14.600
It feels like a –

552
00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:16.580
I think that – I believe that's the technical term.

553
00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:16.780
Yeah.

554
00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:19.420
Do you guys load the super-duper nuke?

555
00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:20.100
Oh, yeah.

556
00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:21.300
It's ready to go.

557
00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:27.900
So, a frantic search followed, but tragically, neither the pilot nor the aircraft was ever recovered.

558
00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:34.480
The only thing they found was his helmet bobbing alone on the surface of the ocean, which really sends chills down your spine.

559
00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:35.760
So, here's the thing.

560
00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:39.980
This was not just a casual cruise that the Ticonderoga was on.

561
00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:48.160
It had just wrapped up combat duty in Vietnam and was en route to Japan for some much-needed rest and refueling.

562
00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:52.720
And that's when the inevitable cover-up begins.

563
00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:55.260
So, let's break down the optics for a second.

564
00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:55.820
The U.S.

565
00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:08.100
aircraft carrier, fresh from war, accidentally drops a nuclear bomb just off the coast of Japan, a country with, let's say, a very specific set of feelings about nuclear bombs.

566
00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:12.760
This is – again, it's 1965, so not too much time has passed.

567
00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:20.880
Oh, and just for some added detail, we had signed a treaty promising we wouldn't bring nukes anywhere near Japanese shores.

568
00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:24.380
So, yeah, this was not going to go over well.

569
00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:29.380
Was the ship more than 200 nautical miles off the coastline of Japan?

570
00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:29.920
Oh, my God.

571
00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:30.480
Who are you?

572
00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:31.080
What kind of –

573
00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:33.060
This is a very specific question.

574
00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:44.420
Well, I had just read that – I had just read that every country is responsible and has ownership of the first 200 nautical miles off their shoreline.

575
00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:52.940
So, if this was within Japan's 200 nautical miles, that is a very big breach because you're not in open ocean.

576
00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:53.320
Yeah.

577
00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:57.460
I believe it was well within Japanese jurisdiction.

578
00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:01.780
It was a very bad look.

579
00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:08.480
And so, of course, to avoid the bad look, the Navy tried to cover it up.

580
00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:11.100
They zipped it up, tight-lipped.

581
00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:17.320
But as we all know, military secrets have a way of slinking into the public zeitgeist eventually.

582
00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:19.620
Are you guys good secret keepers?

583
00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:20.300
Are you trustworthy?

584
00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:21.500
Lock it up.

585
00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:22.400
Lock it up.

586
00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:22.820
Yeah.

587
00:24:23.020 --> 00:24:26.300
But not – this is not a secret I could keep.

588
00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:26.820
Yeah.

589
00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.300
I would be a whistleblower.

590
00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:33.660
I'm good at keeping your secrets or Angela's secrets or friends and family secrets.

591
00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:34.980
My own secrets.

592
00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:43.700
But I'm not going to keep your we-lost-a-nuclear-weapon-in-the-ocean secret for you if you're the military.

593
00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:48.340
Like, I'm going to be the lady who is writing a report on that.

594
00:24:55.000 --> 00:25:02.440
The Navy was spectacularly or perhaps frighteningly able to conceal this incident for over 15 years.

595
00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:06.020
It wasn't until 1981 that the Pentagon disclosed the incident.

596
00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:12.460
The Washington Post reported about it in a mere three sentences buried in a nuclear accidents report.

597
00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:19.280
However, the folks at the Pentagon also did not bother explaining the details of where exactly the bomb was.

598
00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:23.980
They first stated that the bomb was more than 500 miles from land.

599
00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.260
Vague much?

600
00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:32.820
They also assured everyone that the bomb posed no danger since it was 16,000 feet underwater.

601
00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:43.500
But in 1989, the environmental group Greenpeace, alongside naval expert William Arkin, exposed more naval documents,

602
00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:52.640
revealing that the bomb was dropped only 70 miles off the coast of the Ryuku Islands, as opposed to the 500 they had said before.

603
00:25:52.640 --> 00:26:04.020
They also pointed out that this incident violated Japan's strict anti-nuclear policies and exposed some serious Navy secrets about nuclear weapons in Vietnam.

604
00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:12.960
With no other choice, the Pentagon finally admitted the whole truth about this gigantic snafu in a statement that same year in 1989.

605
00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:20.120
Did this create a diplomatic issue for the United States and the Japanese government?

606
00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:21.040
Yeah.

607
00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:22.080
In 1989?

608
00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:23.360
Yeah, for sure.

609
00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:33.400
It feels like these international incidents are—there's always some measure of just sort of posturing of, like, we have to respond this way because protocol demands it of us,

610
00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:39.380
and so we must make these statements, and we must say these things, and we must say that we're outraged, and then the U.S.

611
00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:45.920
must respond with their, like, diplomatic explanation and acceptance of responsibility.

612
00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:48.600
It's like, it's so choreographed, all these things.

613
00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:50.460
It's very wild how it all works out.

614
00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:53.540
And then I always wonder, like, does anyone actually take this personally?

615
00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:57.820
If, like, Japan is expressing outrage, like, how dare they?

616
00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:03.520
Is there any, like, Japanese official who's, like, genuinely pissed, like, at a personal level?

617
00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:07.060
Or is it just sort of, like, grand institutional emotion?

618
00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:13.160
I'm also curious, what happens to a nuclear—a super dupe nuke?

619
00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:14.300
Super duper nuke?

620
00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:15.660
A super duper nuke.

621
00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:17.160
Does it get pressurized?

622
00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:19.320
How deep was the water 70 miles off?

623
00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:20.300
This is a great question.

624
00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:22.500
And Greenpeace had a lot to say about this.

625
00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:23.000
Okay.

626
00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:31.480
So when the Pentagon admitted this in 1989, they were actually responding to an article in Newsweek, and then Japanese papers picked up the story, which, of course,

627
00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:35.480
caused quite a stir throughout Japan itself, rightfully so.

628
00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:40.500
Though the Pentagon confirmed that the bomb would not be harmful—it hadn't been armed,

629
00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:47.540
and you have to go through a whole bevy of procedural elements to actually arm it—it was still 16,000 feet underwater, decaying,

630
00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:54.060
which meant it might eventually break down and start to emit serious pollutants.

631
00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:55.160
Yet, U.S.

632
00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:59.360
spokespersons claimed this wouldn't cause any harm to the natural world.

633
00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:08.520
Either way, it was safe to say they were in the hot seat, at least as hot as the nuclear material inside that bomb, shall we say.

634
00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:19.740
So while many were rightfully mad about the Big Kerplunk, more were outraged because, in fact, the ship shouldn't have had the bomb on it in the first place.

635
00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:28.180
Yes, at the time of the incident, Japan had a ban on nuclear weapons, and there was apparently an unspoken agreement between the two world powers.

636
00:28:28.600 --> 00:28:31.960
Japanese officials would intentionally not ask the U.S.

637
00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:34.300
ships what they were carrying, and the U.S.

638
00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:36.520
wouldn't volunteer any info either.

639
00:28:36.520 --> 00:28:40.480
It was one of those sort of, like, don't ask, don't tell kinds of situations.

640
00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:42.300
So what happened exactly?

641
00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:45.960
The incident fueled further questions about the use of nuclear weaponry in U.S.

642
00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:48.840
combat via diplomatic inquiry from Japan.

643
00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:53.980
It also called into question how we adhere to certain protocols and standards for safety,

644
00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:59.780
especially when it involves high-powered weapons and moving planes around aircraft carriers.

645
00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:03.940
Plus, you know, it solidified the longstanding tradition of lying to the American public.

646
00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:10.340
Since 1950, there have somehow been a whopping 32 broken arrow incidents.

647
00:29:10.700 --> 00:29:12.360
This includes theft.

648
00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:13.040
Wait, wait.

649
00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:14.420
Yeah, raising hands.

650
00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:14.610
What?

651
00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:16.700
32 super duper nukes?

652
00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:17.100
Gone?

653
00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:18.300
Super duper nukers.

654
00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:22.760
32 lost nuclear weapons.

655
00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:24.160
Oy, oy, oy.

656
00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:27.720
Now I know why they call it broken arrows.

657
00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:35.680
Because when you say, you know what, we have 32 broken arrows, you're like, oh, if you say we have 32 lost nuclear weapons, you're like, wait, stop?

658
00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:36.160
What?

659
00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:37.820
Say again?

660
00:29:38.180 --> 00:29:40.620
Euphemisms make hard things a lot easier.

661
00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:41.600
Oh, man.

662
00:29:41.800 --> 00:29:41.940
Yeah.

663
00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:42.400
Yeah.

664
00:29:42.980 --> 00:29:43.300
Yeah.

665
00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:43.880
32.

666
00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:47.960
That's why Greg called all of our talking heads candy bags, you know?

667
00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:48.860
I forgot that.

668
00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:53.560
He said that he always felt bad giving us pages and pages of extra monologues.

669
00:29:53.660 --> 00:29:55.500
So he was like, I'll name them something fun.

670
00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:57.180
So he named them candy bags.

671
00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:58.420
I loved those.

672
00:29:58.660 --> 00:30:06.680
I loved doing those talking heads and, like, coming up with, like, new riffs and, like, the candy bag alts, like, all the different versions of our talking heads.

673
00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:09.960
Like, just, it was like getting a window into the writer's brains.

674
00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:16.500
And I just remember loving the math of those jokes and, like, always trying to think about how to heighten them.

675
00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:18.900
And they'd have to kick me out of those talking heads.

676
00:30:19.080 --> 00:30:22.860
But to your point, naming all the extra work a candy bag.

677
00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:23.440
It's a trick.

678
00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:25.340
Definitely made it go down easier.

679
00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:31.160
And also calling all our screw-ups bloopers makes them sound fun, too.

680
00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:32.500
Like, oh, that was a blooper.

681
00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:35.160
Like, that's just a broken arrow.

682
00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:38.000
I mean, even the word snafu is kind of fun.

683
00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:38.820
It's snafu.

684
00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:40.820
Guys, it's just a snafu.

685
00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:41.500
Oopsie.

686
00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:42.720
Whoopsie doozy.

687
00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:43.380
Yeah.

688
00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:56.400
So these 32 broken arrow incidents include theft, accidental detonation, and just straight up loss, like the one that rolled off the back of the Ticonderoga.

689
00:30:56.600 --> 00:30:56.860
Like.

690
00:30:57.020 --> 00:30:57.200
Yeah.

691
00:30:57.440 --> 00:31:00.100
I don't know, like, which one bothers me the moSt.

692
00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:06.940
I don't know if it's that we lost some, that they were stolen, or accidentally detonated.

693
00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:08.420
Like, they're all three of those.

694
00:31:08.420 --> 00:31:09.680
I don't know which one's worse.

695
00:31:09.960 --> 00:31:11.760
I feel like theft is the scarieSt.

696
00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:12.780
Theft.

697
00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:13.380
Yeah.

698
00:31:14.200 --> 00:31:14.820
I do, too.

699
00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:18.860
But the next time you're feeling clumsy for dropping something, just remember that the U.S.

700
00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:24.540
Navy dropped an entire hydrogen bomb into the ocean and then kicked sand over it for 15 years.

701
00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:25.840
And they got away with it.

702
00:31:25.840 --> 00:31:29.460
So the lesson here, guys, is lie.

703
00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:32.240
If you mess up, lie about it.

704
00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:37.220
Because then 15 years will go by.

705
00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:37.960
That's the lesson.

706
00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:38.920
That's the takeaway.

707
00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:40.700
Because then 15 years will go by.

708
00:31:40.700 --> 00:31:42.860
And then it's like nobody really gets in trouble.

709
00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:44.780
There's a little bit of saber rattling.

710
00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:51.280
And, like, people get, like, governments get mad and exchange sort of diplomatic, you know, zingers at each other.

711
00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:53.260
But it's not really, like, a thing.

712
00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:54.440
So, yeah.

713
00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:55.680
I guess that's the lesson.

714
00:31:55.960 --> 00:31:57.920
Do you think there are any other valuable lessons here, guys?

715
00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:01.040
I think there's a second part to that.

716
00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.280
It would be lie plus time.

717
00:32:03.560 --> 00:32:04.360
Give it time.

718
00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:06.560
Lie for a long time.

719
00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:07.160
Be patient with your lie.

720
00:32:07.160 --> 00:32:13.360
I think what you're saying is hold your lies deep inside of you for a really long time.

721
00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:15.700
Hold those secrets.

722
00:32:16.200 --> 00:32:17.580
Not other people's secrets.

723
00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:18.720
Your secrets.

724
00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:20.500
Hold them deep.

725
00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:22.640
Let them devour you from the inside.

726
00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:24.280
Such a positive message.

727
00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:24.640
I know.

728
00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:25.180
Snap.

729
00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:31.860
But in all seriousness, is there any, like, positive takeaway here?

730
00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:36.900
Like, is there a way to frame this or look at this that, like, gives us a good lesson?

731
00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:38.120
Yeah.

732
00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:39.420
Thank you, Greenpeace.

733
00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:40.120
Amen.

734
00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:41.020
Thank you.

735
00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:41.580
I love it.

736
00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:47.240
Way to stand for something and hold people accountable.

737
00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:51.940
Like, thank goodness you're around because maybe we would have never known.

738
00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:52.920
Right?

739
00:32:53.140 --> 00:32:56.300
Because they seem like they were really sounding the alarm on this.

740
00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:56.760
Yep.

741
00:32:57.280 --> 00:32:58.680
They were pissed.

742
00:32:59.020 --> 00:32:59.400
Yeah.

743
00:32:59.580 --> 00:33:00.680
About all this stuff.

744
00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:01.240
Yeah.

745
00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:01.920
They were pissed.

746
00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:05.580
They were not going to let this just be swept under the rug.

747
00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:10.140
Look, it is hard to believe, like, despite what the Pentagon said, it's like, it's hard

748
00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:15.840
to believe that a nuclear missile just decaying on the bottom of the ocean is, like, completely

749
00:33:15.840 --> 00:33:16.240
harmless.

750
00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:17.840
It's kind of hard to believe.

751
00:33:17.900 --> 00:33:19.200
It doesn't make any logical sense.

752
00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:23.820
I mean, what is that going to do to the ocean and the ecosystem around it?

753
00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:25.640
It's probably going to kill a couple of starfish.

754
00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:28.220
It's going to take out a few coral reefs.

755
00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:36.340
I would also say that what is leaking there, I'm not saying it's this one nuke, but there

756
00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:39.060
are all these studies about what's in our fish.

757
00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:42.460
There's, like, all kinds of scary heavy metals in the fish.

758
00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:42.780
Mm-hmm.

759
00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:50.400
I mean, would you take a dirty penny and put it in a glass of water and let it sit overnight

760
00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:53.380
and then drink that glass of water the next day?

761
00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:54.880
You wouldn't.

762
00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:56.080
You wouldn't.

763
00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:57.400
I mean, come on.

764
00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:59.500
It's doing something down there.

765
00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:01.180
For sure.

766
00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:03.340
Can I just bring us back?

767
00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:03.900
Bring us back.

768
00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:04.940
For a little callback here.

769
00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:10.540
I think instead of broken arrow, they should be called a dirty penny.

770
00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:13.280
Thank you, Angela.

771
00:34:13.460 --> 00:34:13.960
I like that.

772
00:34:14.160 --> 00:34:16.440
Got some dirty pennies to account for, you guys.

773
00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:18.380
Yeah, that's right.

774
00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:21.900
